**What**
Sequence elements are keyed by zero-padded position (`0000`, `0001`, ...). A
position is not an identity, so inserting or removing an element shifts every
subsequent key and Git sees every downstream element as modified.

**Where**
`crates/facet-git-tree/src/ser.rs:623-639` (`serialize_sequence`),
`docs/specification.adoc`, requirement `serialization.design.trees.ordering`.

**Why it matters**
Reproduced. Prepending one ingredient to a four-element list:

```
$ git diff refs/store/recipe/carbonara~1 refs/store/recipe/carbonara
ingredients/0000  -egg        +guanciale
ingredients/0001  -pancetta   +egg
ingredients/0002  -pecorino   +pancetta
ingredients/0003  (new)       +pecorino
```

Four changed paths for one insertion. `git log -p` on a list-heavy entity is
unreadable, `git blame`-style attribution on an element is meaningless, and the
README claim that "the unchanged `title` blob keeps its object id across
versions" - true for structs - quietly does not hold for list *positions*.

The element blobs themselves do dedup, so storage cost is fine. The damage is
entirely to human legibility, which is the thing being sold. The interesting
design observation is that Git already solved this for the analogous case:
`git diff -M` detects renames by content, but it only applies within a diff of
*files*, and here the moved content is the file, so rename detection sees
modifications rather than moves.

**Options**
- Option A - name sequence entries by a content-derived key (e.g. the elements
  own short oid) and record order in a separate `.order` blob. Insertions then
  touch one new entry plus the order blob, and `git diff` shows exactly what
  changed. Trade-off: entry names stop being readable indices, `ls-tree` output
  becomes hex soup, and the order blob is a hot spot that changes on every
  reorder.
- Option B - keep ordinals but sparse them (`0000`, `1000`, `2000`, ...) so an
  insertion can usually take a gap without shifting neighbours, renumbering
  only when a gap runs out. Trade-off: order is no longer a dense index, and
  the amortized renumber still produces an occasional big diff.
- Option C - schema-level element identity: let a `List` node declare a key
  field (`List { elem, key: Option<String> }`), and name entries by that field
  when present. `ingredients` keyed by nothing stays positional; a list of
  structs keyed by `id` gets stable names and clean diffs. Trade-off: an
  addition to the schema format, and a `SchemaDoc` change is semver-major by
  `schema.representation`.
- Option D - accept it and say so. Position *is* the identity of an element in
  a `Vec<String>`; there is no better answer without more information.

**Recommendation**
Option D for bare scalar lists, Option C for lists of records - which is where
this actually hurts, because a list of records is the shape that most wants
per-element history. Medium confidence; Option C is real design work and may be
post-conference. At minimum, be ready to answer this from the audience, because
"list diffs cascade" is the second question a Git person will ask after seeing
the diff demo.